#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060893 :tutorial/content "The derivatives of sine, cosine, and tangent are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin x$ | $\\cos x$ |\n| $\\cos x$ | $-\\sin x$ |\n| $\\tan x$ | $\\sec^2 x$ |\n\nNow that we know the derivatives of the standard trigonometric functions, we can differentiate any combination of them by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of $y = \\cos x + \\sin x.$\n\nHere, we can use the fact that the derivative of the sum is equal to the sum of the derivatives, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll derive the derivatives shown in the table at the end of this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "dca4e1b4-5f89-5c88-9f24-b4671410d644" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2618 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060894 :tutorial/content "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(h)+\\operatorname{sin}(h)\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x)(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a25dc2a-846b-5554-9cc7-682b81f17e0f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2619 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Sine"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060895 :tutorial/content "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(h)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)-\\operatorname{cos}(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x)(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine." :tutorial/id #uuid "40bcba62-6d88-5cfd-9d89-e773514e5a63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2620 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Cosine"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060896 :tutorial/content "We can also prove that $(\\tan x)'= \\sec^2 x$ using limits, though it's a bit cumbersome. \n\nA faster way is to use a rule of differentiation known as the **quotient rule**. \n\nYou'll learn about the quotient rule very soon!" :tutorial/id #uuid "32a1aa14-0e0a-53c1-a40c-e728e4b231c3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2621 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Tangent"}]}
